course: biology
lesson: 52
title: How animals hunt
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: A hunter has to find its food, get close, catch it and hold on, and every hunter has its own plan: some chase, some sneak, some set traps and some simply wait.
explain: The cheetah is the sprinter, the wolf pack is the long-distance team, the python and the praying mantis are the patient ambushers, and the spider and the anglerfish are trap builders.
explain: Watch a pet cat with a toy mouse: it creeps low, freezes, wiggles its bottom, then pounces; that wiggle is the cat testing its footing and getting ready to push off hard, exactly like a lion in the long grass.
explain: Most hunts fail, even for lions, so hunters must be clever with their energy: a cheetah can only sprint for a few seconds, and a python can wait for days because it needs to eat only every few weeks.
explain: Cartoons show the lion as king of the jungle, hunting alone while the lionesses rest; in real life lions live on grassy savannah, and the lionesses do most of the hunting, working together.
fact: A cheetah can reach about 100 km per hour, faster than any other land animal, but only for about 20 to 30 seconds before it overheats and must rest; while it lies panting, hyenas or lions often steal its catch.
fact: In a lion pride the lionesses do most of the hunting, often working together to surround zebras, wildebeest or buffalo, and most lion hunts fail.
fact: Barn owls have soft fringed edges on their feathers that make their flight almost silent, and their ears sit at different heights, so a sound reaches each ear a little differently and they can tell up from down and find a mouse by sound alone.
fact: An orb-weaver spider waits on its sticky web and feels the vibrations of trapped insects through tiny hairs on its legs; for its weight, spider silk is stronger than steel.
fact: A chameleon's tongue shoots out to about twice its body length in a fraction of a second, released like a stretched elastic band, with a sticky cup at the tip.
fact: A kingfisher dives head first with very little splash, and Japan's bullet train nose was redesigned to copy its bill, making it quieter in tunnels; water bends light, so a fish looks to be in a slightly different place from where it really is, and a kingfisher learns to aim right.
fact: A female deep-sea anglerfish has a glowing lure on her head, made by light-giving bacteria, and small fish in the dark swim towards it.
fact: Pythons ambush their prey after waiting very still, squeeze it so it cannot breathe, and swallow it whole; some snakes sense the warmth of animals with heat pits on their faces.
fact: Lying still uses very little energy, so a big python can go weeks between meals, while a tiny shrew burns energy so fast that it must eat every few hours.
fact: Grey wolves hunt in family packs, chasing deer, elk or moose over long distances; they are built for stamina rather than speed, and they often pick out a young, old or sick animal that will tire first and is less likely to hurt them.
fact: Some dolphins and humpback whales blow a ring of bubbles round a shoal so the fish, which will not swim through bubbles, bunch up inside, and dolphins also find fish by echolocation.
fact: Cats are ambush hunters, and even well-fed pet cats still hunt because the urge to chase and pounce is built in.
fact: A praying mantis waits without moving, then grabs insects with spiky front legs in less than a tenth of a second, and it can turn its head to look over its shoulder.
exercise: For how long can a cheetah keep up its top speed? | About 20 to 30 seconds | About 10 minutes or even more | About an hour without stopping | All day long, if it is hungry
exercise: In a lion pride, who does most of the hunting? | The lionesses, often working as a team | The biggest male, alone and mostly at dawn | The cubs, to practise | The oldest lion, at night only
exercise: How does a barn owl fly almost silently? | Soft fringed edges on its feathers break up the air | It flaps only once a minute and glides all the rest of the time | It holds its breath while flying | Its feathers are made of fur
exercise: How does a garden spider know that an insect is caught in its web? | It feels the vibrations through hairs on its legs | It sees the insect with its sharp long-distance eyes | It hears the buzzing with ears on its back | It smells it from the other end of the garden
exercise: What makes a chameleon's tongue so fast? | It is loaded like a stretched elastic band and then let go | It is pushed out by a jet of water squirted from inside its throat | It is thrown out by the chameleon's neck | It is made of a metal-like bone
exercise: What did engineers copy from the kingfisher? | The shape of its bill, for a quieter bullet train | Its shiny blue feathers, for warmer waterproof coats | Its eyes, for better cameras | Its feet, for non-slip shoes
exercise: How does a deep-sea anglerfish catch its food? | It waits with a glowing lure that small fish swim towards | It chases fish at great speed through the dark water for hours | It sucks up mud and sieves out fish | It hunts by sound like a bat
exercise: What makes the anglerfish's lure glow? | Light-giving bacteria living in it | A tiny battery-like stone inside its head | Moonlight reflected down from far above | Electricity sent up from the tip of its tail
exercise: Why does a python at the reptile house never chase its food? | It is an ambush hunter that waits, then strikes fast | It is too lazy and would much rather starve than chase anything | It can only eat dead food | Snakes have no muscles for moving fast
exercise: How do grey wolves usually catch a big deer? | The pack chases it over a long way until it tires | One wolf sprints faster than the deer and catches it alone | They wait inside a hollow tree | They scare it into a river and wait
exercise: Why do dolphins blow a ring of bubbles round a shoal of fish? | The fish will not swim through it, so they bunch up | The bubbles make the fish fall asleep | Dolphins breathe out bubbles whenever they are excited | The bubbles carry the fish up to the surface
exercise: Why does a cat wiggle its bottom before pouncing? | To test its footing and get ready to push off hard | To show the mouse that it is friendly and wants to play | Because its back legs are itchy | To shake off dust from its fur
exercise: How does a praying mantis catch its food? | It waits still and grabs insects with spiky front legs | It spins a sticky web like a spider | It shoots out a long sticky tongue, just like a chameleon | It stings them with its tail
exercise: Which animal is the fastest on land? | The cheetah | The African lion | The grey wolf | The racehorse
exercise: Why do most hunters lose more often than they win? | Prey animals are good at running, hiding and spotting danger | Hunters only bother to try when they are not really hungry | Hunters are always too slow | Prey animals are protected by rangers
exercise: How can some snakes sense a warm mouse in the dark? | They have heat-sensing pits on their faces | They see in the pitch dark with glowing eyes | They hear the mouse's heartbeat with their ears | They feel it moving through the tip of their tail
test: lol in the cartoon the lion's king of the jungle and does all the hunting himself. true? | No, lions live on savannah and lionesses do most hunting | Yes, the males hunt and the lionesses stay home and guard the cubs | Yes, lions hunt only in thick jungle | No, lions only eat food that keepers leave
test: Why can a cheetah not keep running at top speed for a whole minute? | It overheats and tires very quickly | Its claws get blunt after a few seconds | The gazelle always gives up first | Its tail gets tangled
test: After a long chase a cheetah lies panting next to its catch, and hyenas trot up. What often happens next? | The hyenas steal its catch | The hyenas help drag it home | The cheetah chases them off | The hyenas wait politely for scraps
test: Why would having one ear higher than the other help an owl find a squeaking mouse? | The sound reaches each ear a little differently, so it can tell up from down | It hears two mice at once | The high ear is for hunting by day and the low one is for hunting by night | It doesn't help, it's just a funny shape
test: What would happen if a garden spider's web had no sticky threads? | Insects would hit it and fly away again | The spider would catch more flies than ever before | The web would last for ever | Birds would get stuck instead
test: Why does a fish in water look like it is in a slightly different place from where it really is? | Water bends the light coming from the fish | The fish moves the moment you look at it | Your eyes are tired from the sun | Fish make themselves partly see-through
test: Why would a glowing lure be a great trap in the deep sea but not in a sunny pond? | In total darkness light stands out, but in sunlight nobody notices it | Pond fish are scared of all lights, even very small ones, and swim away | Pond water stops light from glowing | Deep-sea fish are all blind
test: Why can a big python go weeks without eating when a tiny shrew must eat every few hours? | Waiting still uses very little energy, while a shrew burns it fast | Pythons store spare food in their long tails, like a camel's hump | Shrews have no stomach | Pythons eat sunlight like plants
test: Why might wolves pick out a young, old or sick deer from a herd? | It will tire first and is less likely to hurt them | Old deer taste the best | The healthy ones are protected by law | Wolves feel sorry for the others and let them go
test: my cat's fed loads but still kills birds. is she just evil or what | No, the urge to chase and pounce is built in, even when full | Yes, she is hunting out of anger | She's hungry, you're just not feeding her enough proper food | She's taking the birds back to her mother
test: Which is true about the praying mantis? | It can turn its head and look over its shoulder | It really prays before it eats | It chases flies across the garden | It has no front legs at all
exam: Identify the principal factor limiting the duration of the cheetah's maximal sprint. | Rapid overheating and exhaustion of the animal | Insufficient grip of the claws upon dry ground | The interference of the tail with the gait | The legal protection afforded to its prey
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the division of hunting within a lion pride? | The lionesses undertake most of the hunting, often in cooperation | The dominant male conducts all hunting unaccompanied, chiefly at night | Hunting is undertaken exclusively by the cubs | Lions procure food solely within dense jungle
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the luminous lure of the female anglerfish. | Its light is produced by bacteria and attracts prey in darkness | It is illuminated by moonlight reflected downward from the surface | It discharges electricity to stun passing vessels | It serves exclusively to frighten larger predators
exam: Ascertain the means by which certain dolphins confine a shoal of fish. | By encircling the shoal with a ring of bubbles | By singing to the fish until they sleep | By stirring mud from the seabed into a solid wall | By lowering the temperature of the water
